{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>Therefore they shall not eat.</b> Like 'and He is not so compared' (i.e., 'therefore' comes to teach a rationale). That is, it is fitting to penalize and punish the children of Israel for eating the sinew of the thigh, because they left their father going alone, as it is written: 'ויותר יעקב לבדו' (and Jacob was left alone). They were mighty men, and they should have waited for their father and assisted him if needed, but they did not give him an escort, and he was harmed because of them. From here onward it shall be a remembrance for them, and they shall be zealous in the commandment of escorting; therefore Jacob escorted Joseph. Another interpretation: <b>Therefore</b> — since Jacob was able to stand against the angel — <b>the children of Israel shall not eat the sinew of the thigh</b>, as a remembrance and a glory for them, that their father wrestled with the angel. Another interpretation: <b>Therefore</b> — since Jacob was injured <b>in the sinew of the thigh</b>, his sons accepted upon themselves from then onward not to eat of it. Like a person who felt pain in his head or in one of his limbs, who takes upon himself never to eat of that limb, so that that limb may serve him for healing. It was taught: they said to Rabbi Yehuda, but was it written 'therefore the children of Jacob shall not eat'? It was written 'the children of Israel,' and they were not called the children of Israel until Sinai. He said to them: it was stated at Sinai and written here, to make known for what reason it was forbidden to them."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:13.845745794Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}